# Optional environment for the gate pass service.
#
#   sudo cp deploy/env.example /opt/gate-pass/.env
#   sudo chown gatepass:gatepass /opt/gate-pass/.env
#   sudo chmod 600 /opt/gate-pass/.env
#   sudo systemctl restart gate-pass
#
# The service starts perfectly well WITHOUT this file — the unit references it
# with a leading "-", so a missing file is not an error. Nothing here is
# required; it exists so a setting can be changed without editing the systemd
# unit, which install.sh overwrites on every update.
#
# NEVER commit the real .env. This repository is public, and .gitignore
# excludes it.

# The key that signs session cookies. Left unset, one is generated into
# storage/secret_key (mode 600) on first run and reused for ever, which is what
# you want. Set it only to keep sessions alive across a REBUILD of the machine,
# where storage/ is not being carried over. Changing it signs everybody out.
# GATE_PASS_SECRET_KEY=

# Marks the session cookie HTTPS-only. deploy/enable-https.sh sets this once a
# certificate is actually live. Setting it by hand on a plain-HTTP site means
# the browser will not keep the cookie and nobody can sign in — the login page
# accepts the password and returns straight to itself, with no error.
# GATE_PASS_HTTPS=1

# WHERE A SECOND COPY OF THE BOOK GOES.
#
# Without this the only backup sits on the same disk as the database it is
# backing up. That protects against a mistake — a bad import, a wrong delete —
# and against nothing else: not the disk failing, not the machine being stolen,
# not the office flooding. This app deletes the source PDFs once a pass is
# issued, so the register is the ONLY copy of the book. Treat it that way.
#
# Space-separated. Each entry is either a mounted directory or anything scp
# understands. backup.sh compares sha256 after copying and fails loudly if a
# copy did not land, because a mirror nobody verified is not a mirror.
#
# A tailnet address is the useful one: it reaches the other machine wherever it
# happens to be, and the second copy should not be in the same building.
#
# Three kinds of destination, and they are not equally reliable:
#
#   rclone:gdrive:gate-pass-backups     cloud storage. The only one that does
#                                       not depend on a device being switched
#                                       on at 9pm. Recommended.
#   /mnt/usb/gate-pass                  a stick or disk left plugged in.
#   fanzart@100.123.71.31:/path         another machine over SSH. A laptop shut
#                                       in a bag receives nothing.
#
# GATE_PASS_BACKUP_MIRRORS="rclone:gdrive:gate-pass-backups"


# SETTING UP GOOGLE DRIVE (about ten minutes, once)
#
#   sudo apt install rclone
#   sudo -u gatepass rclone config
#
#     n) New remote
#     name> gdrive
#     Storage> drive                      (Google Drive)
#     client_id / client_secret>          leave both blank
#     scope> 1                            (full access — it needs to write)
#     Edit advanced config> n
#     Use web browser to automatically authenticate> n     <- IMPORTANT
#
#   That last "n" matters: this is a server with no browser. rclone then prints
#   a command to run on a machine that HAS one — your laptop. Run it there, sign
#   in to the Google account, and paste the token back. Say y to confirm.
#
# Then check it before trusting it:
#
#   sudo -u gatepass rclone lsd gdrive:
#   sudo -u gatepass /opt/gate-pass/deploy/backup.sh
#
# The backup should end with "mirrored to ... (sha256 matches)". That line means
# the copy was read BACK from Drive and matched byte for byte — not merely that
# an upload was accepted.
#
# WHAT THIS PUTS IN SOMEBODY ELSE'S HANDS
#
# The file is the whole register: every gate pass, every customer, and the
# accounts table with its password hashes. The hashes are scrypt, which is slow
# to attack, but the business data is readable by anyone who can open the file.
# It is going into a Google account, so whoever controls that account can read
# it. Use a company account, not a personal one, and put a real password and
# two-factor on it.
#
# For a copy Google cannot read, wrap the remote in rclone's crypt:
#
#   sudo -u gatepass rclone config      -> n) New remote -> Storage> crypt
#     remote> gdrive:gate-pass-backups
#     ...choose a password and WRITE IT DOWN SOMEWHERE THAT IS NOT THIS SERVER
#   GATE_PASS_BACKUP_MIRRORS="rclone:gpcrypt:"
#
# Lose that password and every backup is permanently unreadable — including by
# you, in the emergency they exist for. That is a real trade, not a formality.
# The verification still works either way: reading the file back through the
# crypt layer is exactly what a restore would do.
#
# SIZE: about 21 KB a night, under 2 MB for 90 days. Drive's free tier is 15 GB.
